<section class="brief" aria-labelledby="inbrief">
  <h2 id="inbrief">In brief</h2>
  <p class="lead">We asked ten openly available AI models, small enough to run on one gaming graphics card, to find
  1,000 numbers in recent SEC filings. Every answer had to come with the page and the exact line it was read from, so a
  computer could check it, or to say "not found" when the number wasn't there.</p>
  <div class="stats">
    <div class="stat"><div class="v">{{ R[bm].setups[bs].summary.accuracy.rate | pct }}</div><div class="l">answered correctly with a checkable quote by the best model ({{ bm | name }}, {{ bs | setup | lower }})</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="v bad">{{ (R[sm].setups[ss].summary.confident_wrong.k) }} in 1,000</div><div class="l">wrong answers that still passed every automatic check, at best ({{ sm | name }}, thinking). Most models: {{ R["granite-4.2-8b"].setups.C1.summary.confident_wrong.k }}–{{ R["phi-4"].setups.C2.summary.confident_wrong.k }}</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="v">{{ "%.0f" | format(100 * H.cw_share.scale_or_unit) }}%</div><div class="l">of those wrong-but-passing answers had the right digits in the wrong units ("thousands" vs "millions")</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="v">{{ H.think_cw_sig | length }} of {{ H.with_c3 | length }}</div><div class="l">models with a thinking mode made significantly fewer wrong-but-passing answers with it</div></div>
  </div>
  <ol class="findings">
    <li><b>Small open models can do much of this job, and the gap between them is large.</b> In the plain setup accuracy ranges from {{ H.acc_c1_bottom.rate | pct }} to {{ H.acc_c1_top.rate | pct }}. {{ top | name }} is ahead of every other model by a statistically clear margin.</li>
    <li><b>Demanding a checkable quote works, but it isn't enough on its own.</b> The checker stops most wrong answers. Yet the answers that pass every check include {{ (100 * (1 - H.passp_max)) | round(1) }}–{{ (100 * (1 - H.passp_min)) | round(1) }}% wrong ones, depending on model and setup. Those are the dangerous errors: correct-looking evidence, wrong number.</li>
    <li><b>Those errors are specific and fixable.</b> They are almost all units read wrongly or the neighbouring column (another year or quarter) of the right line, not invented numbers.</li>
    <li><b>Letting the model think helps more than letting it retry.</b> Thinking cut wrong-but-passing answers for every model that has the mode, at {{ "%.0f" | format(H.tok_mult.values() | min) }}–{{ "%.0f" | format(H.tok_mult.values() | max) }} times the generation cost. Telling a model which check failed and letting it retry adds {{ "%.1f" | format(H.retry_gain_min) }}–{{ "%.1f" | format(H.retry_gain_max) }} points of accuracy but never reduces the dangerous errors, and for {{ H.retry_cw_up | length }} models it increases them: told that its quote failed, a model often finds a line that contains its wrong number.</li>
  </ol>
