<article class="body">

<h2 id="abstract">Abstract</h2>
<div class="abstract">
<p>Language models are increasingly used to read financial documents, but a fluent answer is not a verified one. We
ask whether small open models, the kind that run on a single consumer GPU, can extract numbers from SEC filings
<em>with evidence a machine can check</em>: the page and the verbatim line the number was read from. We built
Filing-Evidence, 1,000 questions over 90 recent 10-K and 10-Q filings of 45 US companies, with gold answers taken
from the filers' own inline XBRL tags, in eight kinds of content including segment tables, numbers in prose and
questions whose answer is not on the pages shown. The protocol, metrics and statistics were registered before any
model ran. We evaluated ten instruct models from ten companies (7–21B parameters) on one RTX 4090 in three setups:
plain, check-and-retry, and the model's own thinking mode. Accuracy with checkable evidence ranges from
{{ H.acc_c1_bottom.rate | pct }} to {{ H.acc_c1_top.rate | pct }} in the plain setup; the best result is
{{ R[bm].setups[bs].summary.accuracy.rate | pct }} ({{ bm | name }}). An automatic checker rejects most wrong
answers, yet {{ R[sm].setups[ss].summary.confident_wrong.k }} to {{ R["phi-4"].setups.C2.summary.confident_wrong.k }}
answers per 1,000 pass every check and are still wrong; across the ten models {{ "%.0f" | format(100 * H.cw_share.scale_or_unit) }}%
of these are scale errors and {{ "%.0f" | format(100 * H.cw_share.wrong_period_or_column) }}% the wrong column
of the right line. Thinking reduces such errors significantly for {{ H.think_cw_sig | length }} of
{{ H.with_c3 | length }} models; retrying after a failed check raises accuracy by up to
{{ "%.1f" | format(H.retry_gain_max) }} points but never reduces them. We release the questions, every model answer,
the code and the protocol.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="intro">1 · Introduction</h2>
<p>An analyst who copies a number from a filing can show where it came from. A language model that does the same job
usually can't, or it gives a citation nobody checks. For a model to be trusted with this work its answers have to be
<em>auditable</em>: each number should come with evidence that can be verified quickly, preferably by software.</p>
<p>The question is simple to state. If every answer must name the page and quote the exact line containing the number,
how often are small open models right, how often are they wrong in a way the quote doesn't reveal, and do two cheap,
training-free interventions help: checking the answer automatically and letting the model try again, or switching on
the model's own reasoning mode? We ask it of models that fit on one 24&nbsp;GB consumer GPU, because those are the
models an organisation can run on its own hardware, with its own documents, at low cost.</p>
<p>We registered three research questions before running any model (the full protocol is in the repository):</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>RQ1.</b> How reliably do ~10B-parameter open instruct models extract a requested number from an SEC filing with evidence that can be checked automatically, and answer "not found" when the number isn't on the pages provided?</li>
  <li><b>RQ2.</b> Where do they fail: in which kinds of filing content, and with which kinds of error?</li>
  <li><b>RQ3.</b> How much do (a) automatic checking with one retry and (b) the model's own thinking mode help, and at what cost in tokens and time?</li>
</ul>
<p>Our working thesis was that a small open model on one consumer GPU can be a trustworthy reader of SEC filings
<em>if</em> every answer must carry evidence a machine can check. The results support the first half of that
sentence more than the second: requiring checkable evidence makes most errors visible, but a specific class of
errors passes through it. Section 7 discusses what closes that gap.</p>

<h2 id="design">2 · Benchmark design</h2>
<figure>
  <div class="canvas">{{ dg.pipeline() }}</div>
  <figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> How the benchmark was built and run. Everything above the line was finished, reviewed and fingerprinted before any model saw a question.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>2.1 Filings and answer key</h3>
<p>We took the most recent 10-K and 10-Q filed on or before 15 November 2026 by 45 large US companies (90 filings):
forty in nine sector groups chosen to cover different statement layouts (technology, banks, other financials,
insurers, REITs, utilities, healthcare, consumer, industrials and others), plus five added for segment coverage.
Banks, insurers, REITs and utilities are included deliberately, because their statements use line items and layouts
unlike an industrial company's.</p>
<p>Gold answers come from the filings' own <b>inline XBRL</b> tags: the machine-readable layer that filers embed in the
document to label each number with its concept, period, unit, scale and dimensions. We use only tags whose number is
<em>displayed</em> on a page, and record the page and the line where it is shown. The answer key is therefore the
filer's own statement, not our annotation.</p>

<h3>2.2 Questions</h3>
<p>Each question names an item, a company and a period in words ("What was Apple Inc.'s total liabilities as of June 27,
2026?") and comes with three pages of the filing: the page that shows the answer, an adjacent page and a random
distractor, in random order. Questions fall into eight <b>slices</b> of 125 each:</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Slice</th><th>What it tests</th><th class="n">Companies</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
  {% for s, label in d.slices.items() %}
  <tr><td><b>{{ s }}</b></td><td>{{ label }}</td><td class="n">{{ d.dataset.slices[s].companies }}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>S8 asks for something that is <em>not</em> on the pages provided: a period not shown, an item the company doesn't
report, a quarter asked of an annual report, or an item that is elsewhere in the filing but not on these pages. The
correct answer is "not found". Strict rules decide which facts can become questions: the number must be a column
figure on a row with a printed label, the row must be fully populated, the period's year must be visible on the page,
and the question names the item by its printed row label when that label is unique on the shown pages.</p>

<h3>2.3 Quality control and freeze</h3>
<p>Before any model ran, 100 questions (stratified across slices) were reviewed one by one against the rendered filing by an AI assistant, under the author's direction, in
each round; the protocol required at least 98 correct. Rounds one to three failed on wording (87, 76 and 96 of 100),
never on gold values: all 400 gold values reviewed across the four rounds were judged right. Round four passed with 98 of
100, and the dataset was frozen with a SHA-256 fingerprint
(<code>{{ d.frozen.fingerprint[:16] }}…</code>). Every result cites it, and the published data verifies against it.</p>

<h3>2.4 The checker</h3>
<p>Models answer in a fixed JSON format: the value, its scale ("in millions"), the page number and the line quoted from
the page, or <code>{"status": "not_found"}</code>. The checker then applies four automatic tests (Figure 2) and
compares the value with the answer key. The sign is not graded, because filers display negative numbers in
inconsistent ways; values are compared after scale, and per-share amounts and percentages as displayed.</p>
<figure>
  <div class="canvas">{{ dg.checker() }}</div>
  <figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> Four checks, then the answer key. The checker sees only the answer and the pages, never the gold, so it could run in production. Its most important outcome is the one it can't detect: <b>confident-wrong</b>, a wrong value whose evidence passes every check.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Every answer receives exactly one outcome. <b>Accuracy</b> is the share of the 875 answerable questions (S1–S7) answered
correctly <em>with evidence that passes the checks</em>: a right number with an unusable quote does not count.
<b>Confident-wrong rate</b> is the share of all 1,000 answers that pass every check but are wrong, and is the metric
that matters most for a system that would act on answers the checker accepts. <b>Abstention accuracy</b> is the share
of S8 questions answered "not found". Every rate carries a 95% Wilson interval; differences are tested with paired
exact McNemar tests on the same questions and Holm-corrected within each family of comparisons.</p>

<h2 id="setup">3 · Models and setups</h2>
<p>Ten general instruct models, each from a different company, all served with vLLM 0.33.0 on one NVIDIA RTX 4090 (24&nbsp;GB) at temperature 0, with
each model's own chat template and its exact Hugging Face revision pinned. Models are used as published: no
fine-tuning, no finance-specific models, no prompt tuning per model; the prompt is identical for every model.
Qwen3-8B, the model of the pilot study that motivated the benchmark, was run as a supplementary baseline and is
reported separately.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Company</th><th class="n">Params</th><th>Precision</th><th>Released</th><th>Thinking mode</th><th>Licence</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
  {% for m in d.ten + d.supplementary %}
  {% set r = R[m] %}
  <tr><td><a href="/research/filing-evidence/{{ m | slug }}">{{ m | name }}</a>{% if r.supplementary %} <span class="muted">(suppl.)</span>{% endif %}</td><td>{{ r.company }}</td><td class="n">{{ "%.1f" | format(r.meta.params / 1e9) }}B</td><td>{{ r.precision }}</td><td class="mono">{{ r.meta.created[:7] }}</td><td>{{ "yes" if r.has_c3 else "—" }}</td><td>{{ LICENCE[r.meta.license_name or r.meta.license] }}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p class="muted">Parameters as published (Qwen3.5-9B and GLM-4.6V-Flash include a vision encoder that was not loaded;
gpt-oss-20b is a mixture-of-experts model with about 3.6B parameters active per token). "Released" is the month
the Hugging Face repository was created. FP8 is weight quantisation by vLLM at load, used where bf16 weights would
not fit; gpt-oss-20b uses its native MXFP4 weights.</p>
<figure>
  <div class="canvas">{{ dg.setups() }}</div>
  <figcaption><b>Figure 3.</b> The three setups. C2 reuses C1's first answer, so the two differ only by the retry. gpt-oss-20b has no non-reasoning mode; its C1 and C2 use the lowest reasoning effort, C3 the highest.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="rq1">4 · RQ1: How reliable are they?</h2>
<figure>
  <div class="canvas">{{ figs.frontier | safe }}</div>
  <figcaption><b>Figure 4.</b> Accuracy against confident-wrong rate for each model in each of its setups (pale: plain; mid: check + retry; dark: thinking). The ideal model sits in the top-left corner. Hover a point for its values.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Accuracy in the plain setup spans a wide range, from {{ H.acc_c1_bottom.rate | pct }} for {{ H.c1_sorted[-1] | name }}
to {{ H.acc_c1_top.rate | pct }} ({{ H.acc_c1_top | ci }}) for {{ top | name }}. The paired tests separate the ten
models into clear tiers (Figure 6): {{ top | name }} is significantly more accurate than every other model; gpt-oss-20b,
Granite-4.2-8B and GLM-4.6V-Flash cannot be told apart from each other; then come Ministral-3-8B, a group of
Gemma-3-12B, Llama-3.1-8B and Phi-4, and finally Nemotron-Nano-9B-v2 and OLMo-3-7B. The best single result is
{{ R[bm].setups[bs].summary.accuracy.rate | pct }} ({{ bm | name }}, {{ bs | setup | lower }}).</p>
<figure>
  <div class="canvas">{{ figs.leader | safe }}</div>
  <figcaption><b>Figure 5.</b> Accuracy with 95% Wilson intervals, per model and setup, ordered by the plain setup.</figcaption>
  <details><summary>Show the data</summary>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Model</th>{% for s in ["C1", "C2", "C3"] %}<th class="n">{{ s }} accuracy</th><th class="n">{{ s }} conf.-wrong</th>{% endfor %}<th class="n">Abstention (C1)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {% for m in H.c1_sorted %}
    <tr><td>{{ m | name }}</td>
    {% for s in ["C1", "C2", "C3"] %}
      {% if s in R[m].setups %}{% set su = R[m].setups[s].summary %}
      <td class="n">{{ su.accuracy.rate | pct }} <span class="ci">{{ su.accuracy | ci }}</span></td><td class="n">{{ su.confident_wrong.k }}</td>
      {% else %}<td class="n">n/a</td><td class="n">n/a</td>{% endif %}
    {% endfor %}
    <td class="n">{{ R[m].setups.C1.summary.abstention_accuracy.k }}/125</td></tr>
    {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  </details>
</figure>
<figure>
  <div class="canvas">{{ figs.sig | safe }}</div>
  <figcaption><b>Figure 6.</b> Which differences in plain-setup accuracy are real. Each cell compares the row model with the column model on the same 875 questions (paired exact McNemar, Holm-corrected over 45 pairs). Grey cells: the data cannot separate the two.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p><b>The checker is the reason these accuracy figures mean something.</b> An answer only counts when its quote is on the
cited page and contains the number. Without that requirement the figures would be higher for every model: a right
number with an unusable quote is common for weaker models (Figure 7). For a reader of the output that difference is
the whole point: a right number you can't verify is not much more useful than a wrong one.</p>
<figure>
  <div class="canvas">{{ figs.outcomes | safe }}</div>
  <figcaption><b>Figure 7.</b> What happens to the 875 answerable questions in the plain setup. Left of the grey band, the model was right; right of it, wrong. The dark red segment is the one that matters most: wrong answers that pass every check.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p><b>"Not found" is handled well by most models.</b> Abstention accuracy in the plain setup ranges from
{{ H.abst_min | pct }} to {{ H.abst_max | pct }}: models rarely invent a number for something that isn't on the
page. Most invented answers come from one kind of trap: a <b>quarterly figure asked of an annual report</b>, which
the filing can't contain. Gemma-3-12B, the weakest abstainer among the ten
({{ R["gemma-3-12b"].setups.C1.summary.abstention_accuracy.rate | pct }}), answered
{{ d.s8["gemma-3-12b:C1"].c[0] }} of those {{ d.s8["gemma-3-12b:C1"].c[1] }} correctly, and the supplementary
Qwen3-8B only {{ d.s8["qwen3-8b:C1"].c[0] }}.</p>

<h2 id="rq2">5 · RQ2: Where do they fail?</h2>
<h3>5.1 By kind of content</h3>
<figure>
  <div class="canvas">{{ figs.heatmap | safe }}</div>
  <figcaption><b>Figure 8.</b> Plain-setup accuracy by slice (S8: correct "not found"). Darker is better.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The main statements are the easiest content: pooled over the ten models, {{ H.slices_c1.S1 | pct }} of current-period
questions are answered correctly. Accuracy falls on prior-period columns ({{ H.slices_c1.S2 | pct }}), in notes
({{ H.slices_c1.S4 | pct }}), and most of all for <b>segments</b> ({{ H.slices_c1.S5 | pct }}), <b>numbers in prose</b>
({{ H.slices_c1.S6 | pct }}) and <b>units and per-share</b> figures ({{ H.slices_c1.S7 | pct }}). Even
{{ top | name }}, strong everywhere else, answers only {{ R[top].setups.C1.summary.by_slice.S6.rate | pct }} of prose
questions correctly. These are the places where a filing's layout stops doing the work: a segment table's columns are
business lines rather than years, and a number in a sentence has no column header at all.</p>

<h3>5.2 What the dangerous errors are</h3>
<figure>
  <div class="canvas">{{ figs.cwmix | safe }}</div>
  <figcaption><b>Figure 9.</b> The confident-wrong answers of each model in the plain setup, by kind of error. Scale or unit: the right digits with the wrong multiplier. Wrong period or column: another figure from the right line. Wrong item: a different line.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Across the ten models there are {{ H.cw_total_c1 | thou }} confident-wrong answers in the plain setup. Only
{{ "%.0f" | format(100 * H.cw_share.wrong_item) }}% are a wrong item. The rest are near misses on the right line:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Scale or unit ({{ "%.0f" | format(100 * H.cw_share.scale_or_unit) }}%).</b> The model quotes the correct line and gives the correct digits, but states the wrong scale, most often "none" for a table headed "(In millions)", or reads a share count as shares rather than millions of shares. The checker can't catch this, because the quote is genuine and contains the number; the units are stated once, at the top of the table.</li>
  <li><b>Wrong period or column ({{ "%.0f" | format(100 * H.cw_share.wrong_period_or_column) }}%).</b> The model quotes the right line and takes the figure for another year, quarter or year-to-date column. The quote again contains the number, so it passes.</li>
</ul>
<p>Scale errors are concentrated in a few models: they are {{ d.cw_split["phi-4:C1"].scale_or_unit }} of Phi-4's
{{ R["phi-4"].setups.C1.summary.confident_wrong.k }} confident-wrong answers, but {{ d.cw_split["qwen3.5-9b:C1"].get("scale_or_unit", 0) }}
of {{ top | name }}'s {{ R[top].setups.C1.summary.confident_wrong.k }}. The secondary metric <em>accuracy ignoring
scale</em> shows how much of each gap is units rather than finding the number: for Phi-4 it is
{{ R["phi-4"].setups.C1.summary.accuracy_ignoring_scale.rate | pct }} against an accuracy of
{{ R["phi-4"].setups.C1.summary.accuracy.rate | pct }}.</p>

