    </dl>
  </div>
  <figcaption><b>Figure 10.</b> A real confident-wrong answer from the strongest model in the plain setup. Every check passes, because the quoted line is genuine and contains the number; only the column is wrong. Of the ten models on this question, {{ ex.others.values() | select("equalto", "correct") | list | length }} answered correctly.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="rq3">6 · RQ3: Retry or think?</h2>
<figure>
  <div class="canvas">{{ figs.effects | safe }}</div>
  <figcaption><b>Figure 11.</b> Change from the plain setup, per model. Left: accuracy (points). Right: confident-wrong answers per 1,000 (negative is better). Filled: significant after Holm correction across all within-model comparisons.</figcaption>
</figure>
<h3>6.1 Check + retry</h3>
<p>When an answer fails a check, the model is told which one ("the quoted line does not appear on page 54") and answers
once more; it is never told the answer. This raises accuracy by {{ "%.1f" | format(H.retry_gain_min) }} to
{{ "%.1f" | format(H.retry_gain_max) }} points, significantly for {{ H.retry_sig | length }} of the ten models, at
little cost: only answers that fail a check are asked again ({{ (100 * R["qwen3.5-9b"].setups.C2.summary.retry_rate.rate) | round(0) | int }}–{{ (100 * R["olmo-3-7b"].setups.C2.summary.retry_rate.rate) | round(0) | int }}% of answers, depending on the model).</p>
<p>But retrying <b>never reduces</b> confident-wrong answers, and for {{ H.retry_cw_up | length }} models
({{ H.retry_cw_up | map("name") | join(", ") }}) it significantly increases them. The mechanism is visible in the
answers: told that its quote failed, a model often keeps its wrong number and finds a line that contains it, which
now passes. For Phi-4, {{ H.caught_to_cw["phi-4"] }} of {{ H.retried["phi-4"] }} retried answers moved from
"caught" to "confident-wrong" in this way. A retry teaches the model to satisfy the checker, not to be right.
Nemotron-Nano-9B-v2 shows the opposite failure: asked to retry, it answered "not found" almost every time.</p>

<h3>6.2 Thinking</h3>
<p>Five of the ten models have a reasoning mode (as does the supplementary Qwen3-8B). Thinking improved accuracy for {{ H.think_gain.values() | select("gt", 0) | list | length }} of them, by up to
{{ "%.1f" | format(H.think_gain["glm-4.6v-flash"]) }} points (GLM-4.6V-Flash), and reduced confident-wrong answers for
{{ ("all " ~ (H.with_c3 | length)) if H.think_cw_all_down else "most" }}: {% for m in H.with_c3 %}{{ m | name }} {{ H.think_cw[m][0] }}→{{ H.think_cw[m][1] }}{{ ", " if not loop.last else "" }}{% endfor %} answers per 1,000 ({{ H.think_cw_sig | length }} of these reductions are significant). The lowest confident-wrong rate in
the study is {{ sm | name }} with thinking: {{ R[sm].setups[ss].summary.confident_wrong.k }} in 1,000.</p>
<p>The exception is {{ "qwen3.5-9b" | name }}, the strongest plain model, whose accuracy fell from
{{ q35.setups.C1.summary.accuracy.rate | pct }} to {{ q35.setups.C3.summary.accuracy.rate | pct }} with thinking. The
cause is the token budget, not the reasoning: it reasoned at length (mean {{ q35.setups.C3.tokens.mean | round(0) | int | thou }}
tokens) and hit the pre-registered 8,192-token limit on {{ q35.setups.C3.tokens.truncated }} questions, leaving
{{ q35.setups.C3.truncated_outcomes.invalid }} with no parseable answer. On the questions it finished, its accuracy was
{{ q35.setups.C3.acc_untruncated.rate | pct }}.</p>
<figure>
  <div class="canvas">{{ figs.cost | safe }}</div>
  <figcaption><b>Figure 12.</b> The cost of thinking: mean generated tokens per answer (log scale) against accuracy. * supplementary baseline.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Thinking is expensive. It multiplies generated tokens by {% for m in H.with_c3 %}{{ "%.0f" | format(H.tok_mult[m]) }}×{{ " (" ~ (m | name) ~ ")" }}{{ ", " if not loop.last else "" }}{% endfor %}.
On one RTX 4090 with 16 concurrent requests, a full 1,000-question run took
{{ R["glm-4.6v-flash"].setups.C3.wall_minutes | round(0) | int }} minutes for GLM-4.6V-Flash with thinking and
{{ R["granite-4.2-8b"].setups.C3.wall_minutes | round(0) | int }} for Granite-4.2-8B, against 3–7 minutes in the plain setup.</p>

<h2 id="discussion">7 · Discussion</h2>
<p><b>Checkable evidence is necessary, not sufficient.</b> Requiring a verbatim quote turned most errors into visible,
rejectable ones: the checker is cheap, needs no model and no gold answer, and could run in any system that uses these
models. What it cannot do is check that the number is the <em>right</em> figure on the line, or that its units are
right. The residue is small for the best configurations ({{ R[sm].setups[ss].summary.confident_wrong.rate | pct }} of
answers at best) and substantial for most (up to {{ R["phi-4"].setups.C2.summary.confident_wrong.rate | pct }}).</p>
<p><b>The residue is structured.</b> {{ "%.0f" | format(100 * (H.cw_share.scale_or_unit + H.cw_share.wrong_period_or_column)) }}% of confident-wrong answers are the right line read wrongly: the
units header misapplied, or the neighbouring column taken. Both are checkable in principle. A stronger checker could
require the model to quote the column header and the units statement as well as the line, and verify that the chosen
figure sits under the quoted header; a units check could compare the stated scale with the table's own heading.
These are engineering steps, not research problems, and they are the natural next version of the benchmark.</p>
<p><b>Retrying is not a free win.</b> Feeding a model the name of the failed check improves accuracy a little, but it
also turns some caught errors into uncaught ones. A system that retries should treat a passed retry with more
suspicion than a passed first answer, or re-verify it more strictly.</p>
<p><b>Model choice matters more than setup.</b> The spread between the best and worst model in the plain setup is
{{ "%.0f" | format(100 * (H.acc_c1_top.rate - H.acc_c1_bottom.rate)) }} points; the largest gain from any setup is about
{{ "%.0f" | format(H.think_gain["glm-4.6v-flash"]) }}. And the strongest model in the plain setup,
{{ top | name }}, reaches {{ R[top].setups.C2.summary.accuracy.rate | pct }} with no reasoning at all.</p>

<h2 id="limits">8 · Limitations</h2>
<ul>
  <li><b>One prompt, temperature 0, one run.</b> Results are for a single fixed prompt shared by all models; a prompt tuned per model could change rankings. Each configuration was run once.</li>
  <li><b>US large-cap filings, text only.</b> 45 companies, recent 10-K and 10-Q filings. No small caps, foreign filers, scanned documents, charts or images.</li>
  <li><b>Three pages per question.</b> The model is shown the right page plus two others; finding the page in a whole filing is a harder, separate task.</li>
  <li><b>Units stated off-page.</b> After the results we found {{ H.n_noheader }} answerable questions ({{ "%.1f" | format(100 * H.n_noheader / 875) }}%) whose page shows no units statement, because the filer states it once on an earlier page. The frozen dataset was not changed. Excluding them moves no model's accuracy by more than {{ "%.1f" | format(100 * H.sens_max) }} points. The plain and check-and-retry rankings do not change; with thinking, Granite-4.2-8B moves ahead of GLM-4.6V-Flash (Appendix A).</li>
  <li><b>Quality control was AI-assisted.</b> The review of the 100-question samples was performed by an AI assistant under the author's direction. The author did not independently verify the full sample.</li>
  <li><b>Error categories were not reviewed by hand.</b> The protocol planned a hand review of 20 answers per model to confirm the automatic error categories; it was not carried out (recorded as a deviation). Outcomes themselves are exact, by construction; the categories are rule-based.</li>
  <li><b>Thinking budget.</b> The 8,192-token reasoning limit was fixed in advance; it truncated {{ q35.setups.C3.tokens.truncated }} of {{ "qwen3.5-9b" | name }}'s and {{ R["gpt-oss-20b"].setups.C3.tokens.truncated }} of gpt-oss-20b's answers. A larger budget would likely raise their thinking-mode results.</li>
  <li><b>Contamination.</b> Models may have seen older filings in training. 89 of the 90 filings were filed in 2026, and eight of the ten models were released before 2026. A memorised answer must still come with a verbatim quote from the pages shown.</li>
  <li><b>Precision.</b> Phi-4 and Gemma-3-12B were run with FP8 weights to fit in 24&nbsp;GB, and gpt-oss-20b in its native MXFP4; results at full precision may differ slightly.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="repro">9 · Reproducibility</h2>
<p>Everything needed to check or repeat this study is public. The code, the protocol (with its deviations log) and
each run's summary are on <a href="{{ GITHUB }}">GitHub</a>; the questions, page texts, every model reply including
its reasoning, and the server logs are on <a href="{{ HF }}">Hugging Face</a>. Each run records the model revision,
the exact vLLM command, the prompt hash and the dataset fingerprint.</p>
<pre><code>git clone {{ GITHUB }}.git && cd filing-evidence
uv sync --all-extras
uv run python hf_data.py pull                       # data, verified against the frozen fingerprint
uv run python runner.py qwen3.5-9b C1 --regrade     # re-grade stored replies (no GPU)
bash run_all.sh qwen3.5-9b                          # rerun a model (one 24 GB GPU)</code></pre>
<p>Re-grading every stored reply reproduces every number on this page. These pages are generated from the stored
results by <code>report/build.py</code> in the same repository.</p>

